    "The issue involves collaborative optimization between the retriever and the large language model (LLM) in Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems and may belong to a relatively new research direction.",
    "Adapter mechanisms involve inserting small trainable modules (i.e., adapters) between certain layers of a large language model and fine-tuning only these modules instead of the entire model to adapt to specific tasks or input distributions [1].",
    "Alignment of retrieved content with LLM preferences can be achieved by using a lightweight adapter to re-rank, summarize, or transform the style of retrieved documents before they are input into the LLM, making them more consistent with the LLM's input preferences formed during pre-training or instruction fine-tuning (e.g., conciseness, structured format, keyword prominence) [2].",
    "Some works propose parameterizing the interface between the retriever and the LLM (e.g., using adapters or projection layers) and performing end-to-end training to make retrieved content more 'friendly' to the LLM’s comprehension capabilities [3].",
    "Suggested keywords for further literature search include 'adapter-based alignment in RAG', 'retriever-LLM interface optimization', and 'modular adaptation for retrieval-augmented LMs'."
