    "The RAG workflow includes data preprocessing, document indexing, query understanding, query embedding, retrieval, document re-ranking (optional), context fusion, generation, post-processing, and result output.",
    "Data preprocessing involves cleaning raw data, removing irrelevant content, formatting, tokenization, and removing stop words.",
    "Document indexing involves splitting processed data into chunks, converting text chunks into vector form using an encoder (such as a Transformer Encoder), and storing these vectors in a retrievable database.",
    "Query understanding involves interpreting the user's question or request to grasp its intent.",
    "Query embedding uses the same encoder as in the indexing phase to convert the user query into vector form.",
    "Retrieval uses the query vector to find the most relevant document chunks from the indexed database, typically using similarity metrics like cosine similarity.",
    "Document re-ranking is an optional step that reorders retrieved document chunks to optimize relevance.",
    "Context fusion combines the retrieved document chunks with the original query to form enriched contextual information.",
    "Generation uses a generative model, such as a large language model, to produce a response or text based on the fused context.",
    "Post-processing includes grammar checking and error correction to enhance text quality.",
    "Result output delivers the final generated text as a response to the user.",
    "Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) integrates information retrieval and generative models to assist large language models (LLMs) in producing more accurate and contextually appropriate answers by leveraging external knowledge bases.",
    "When generating text or answering questions, a large language model using RAG first retrieves relevant information from a large document collection and then uses that information to guide text generation.",
    "The RAG architecture consists of three main modules: Indexer, Retriever, and Generator.",
