Statements:
[
    "In the Collapsed Gibbs Sampling method for LDA models, 'Collapsed' refers to marginalizing out the parameters—the document-topic distribution \theta_m and the topic-word distribution \varphi_k—by integration.",
    "The method directly samples the topic assignment z for each word instead of estimating \theta_m and \varphi_k.",
    "This approach simplifies computation by avoiding direct inference on high-dimensional continuous parameters.",
    "Sampling is performed only in the discrete topic space.",
    "After sampling, \theta_m and \varphi_k are analytically recovered through frequency statistics.",
    "'Collapsed' means removing the parameters \theta_m and \varphi_k from the joint distribution via integration during Bayesian inference.",
    "\theta_m represents the topic distribution for each document.",
    "\varphi_k represents the word distribution for each topic.",
    "The model focuses on the latent variable z, which denotes the topic assigned to each word.",
    "Sampling only the discrete topic labels z reduces computational complexity compared to sampling continuous distributions.",
    "Once all word-topic assignments z are determined, \theta_m and \varphi_k can be computed analytically using counts and Dirichlet priors.",
    "This method avoids iterative optimization for parameter estimation.",
    "The approach can be viewed as a form of stochastic EM.",
    "In this stochastic EM interpretation, the E-step approximates the posterior distribution via sampling.",
    "The M-step is deferred until the end and performed only once.",
    "This avoids the repeated M-step updates in traditional EM, improving efficiency."
] 

